The RBF kernel corresponds to an inner product in an infinite-dimensional space, making it highly effective for capturing complex patterns in data.1 Using this kernel trick involves computing the kernel matrix K, where Kij = K(xi, xj). This results in an N × N matrix, which is computationally prohibitive and impractical in CL. Instead, we approximate the mapping φ(x) using Random Fourier Features and work directly with these finite-dimensional features to avoid computing or storing K.
